INTERVIEW_SETS = [
    ("Transport and daily routines", [
        ("How do you usually travel to your classes, and what do you like about that method?", ["travel", "classes", "because"], "Describe your usual journey and one concrete benefit."),
        ("Tell me about a time when a travel delay affected your plans. How did you respond?", ["delay", "plan", "time"], "Describe the delay, your response, and the outcome."),
        ("Would you prefer a cheaper but longer commute or a shorter but more expensive one? Explain your choice.", ["commute", "cost", "time"], "Choose an option and relate the trade-off to your routine."),
        ("A university wants to reduce private car use. What policy would you recommend, and what difficulty might it face?", ["university", "transport", "students"], "Suggest a policy, explain its benefit, and address one implementation difficulty."),
    ]),
    ("Learning with technology", [
        ("What digital tool do you use most often for studying, and how do you use it?", ["tool", "study", "use"], "Identify a tool and describe a specific learning activity."),
        ("Describe a time when technology failed during an important task. What did you do next?", ["technology", "problem", "task"], "Give an incident, an alternative action, and a result."),
        ("Do you prefer taking notes on paper or on a device? Explain how your choice affects your learning.", ["notes", "learning", "prefer"], "Connect a clear preference with an example of learning."),
        ("Should universities require students to use a single digital learning platform? Consider a benefit and a possible drawback.", ["platform", "students", "university"], "Balance consistency with a practical limitation and give a recommendation."),
    ]),
    ("Working with a community", [
        ("What kind of community activity would you enjoy joining, and why?", ["community", "activity", "because"], "Name an activity and explain your personal interest."),
        ("Tell me about a time you helped another person solve a problem. What was your role?", ["help", "problem", "person"], "Explain the situation and your contribution with an outcome."),
        ("Would you rather volunteer regularly for one organization or try several short projects? Give reasons.", ["volunteer", "projects", "regular"], "Choose a pattern of volunteering and explain its advantages."),
        ("Some universities require community service for graduation. What effects might this have on students and local organizations?", ["service", "students", "organizations"], "Consider both groups and distinguish a possible benefit from a difficulty."),
    ]),
    ("Culture and leisure", [
        ("What do you usually do to relax after a demanding week of study?", ["relax", "study", "week"], "Describe a familiar activity and why it helps."),
        ("Describe a cultural event that made a strong impression on you. What did you take away from it?", ["event", "learned", "experience"], "Give a specific event and explain its impact."),
        ("Would you prefer to attend a live performance or watch a recording at home? Explain your preference.", ["live", "recording", "prefer"], "Compare the experiences and justify your choice."),
        ("A city has limited funds for cultural activities. Should it support large festivals or small neighborhood programs? Explain your recommendation.", ["city", "programs", "community"], "Make a choice, consider access or cost, and support it with an example."),
    ]),
]

# Each email has its own situation and developed model response.
EMAILS = [
    ('Your project group reserved a room for a presentation rehearsal. The room’s screen is broken, and your presentation depends on showing visual material to the whole group. You would like the coordinator to help without assuming that any room will meet your needs.\n\nWrite an email to the room coordinator. In your email:\n- Identify the booking and explain the screen problem.\n- Describe the equipment or room features your group needs.\n- Propose a practical replacement or other solution.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the room coordinator\nSubject: Equipment for our rehearsal', ["room", "screen", "rehearsal"],
     "Dear Room Coordinator,\nOur group reserved Room 16 for a presentation rehearsal on Thursday afternoon, but the screen did not turn on when we checked it today. We need to display several detailed graphs so that everyone can practice explaining the same evidence. Could you move our reservation to a room with a working display? A space for six students would be sufficient, and we could begin an hour later if necessary. If no room is available, borrowing a portable projector would also help. Please let me know which option is possible before Thursday morning so I can inform the group. Thank you for your assistance.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You registered for library training because you need to use its resources for a course project. A required class now takes place at the same time as the training. You do not want to miss the training entirely and can attend at other times.\n\nWrite an email to the librarian. In your email:\n- Explain the timetable conflict.\n- Request another way to complete the training.\n- Give your availability so the librarian can suggest an arrangement.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the librarian\nSubject: Alternative library training session', ["training", "class", "session"],
     "Dear Librarian,\nI am registered for the research-database training on Tuesday at two, but my department has just scheduled a required laboratory class at the same time. I would still like to complete the training because I need to find reliable sources for my first research paper. Is there another session later this week? I am available after three on Wednesday and throughout Friday morning. If those sessions are full, I would be grateful for a place on the waiting list or access to equivalent recorded instruction. I apologize for changing my booking at short notice and appreciate your help finding a practical alternative.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('A campus service webpage lists opening hours that do not match the information at the service desk. You relied on the webpage and were unable to complete an important visit. You want the manager to check the discrepancy and prevent similar problems.\n\nWrite an email to the service manager. In your email:\n- Identify the conflicting information.\n- Explain how the discrepancy affected you.\n- Ask for confirmation of the correct hours and an appropriate update.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the service manager\nSubject: Conflicting opening hours', ["hours", "website", "update"],
     "Dear Service Manager,\nI am writing about the opening hours listed for the equipment desk on the campus website. The page says that the desk stays open until six on Fridays, but the sign at the desk states that it closes at four. I arrived at five yesterday and could not collect a recorder needed for weekend fieldwork. Could you confirm the current hours and update the webpage if the sign is correct? Adding the change to existing reservation messages would also help students who booked earlier. I understand schedules sometimes change, but consistent information would prevent unnecessary journeys and missed collections. Thank you for checking this.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('A guest lecturer presented an idea that may be useful for your class project. You would like to read more, but you are not an expert in the lecturer’s field. A focused request would help the lecturer recommend a source at the right level.\n\nWrite an email to the guest lecturer. In your email:\n- Mention a specific idea from the lecture.\n- Explain its connection to your interests or project.\n- Request a suitable reading and describe the level of guidance you need.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the guest lecturer\nSubject: Reading related to your lecture', ["lecture", "reading", "urban"],
     "Dear Dr. Chen,\nThank you for your lecture on urban wetlands yesterday. I was particularly interested in your explanation that a restored site's water movement matters as much as its visible vegetation. My class project compares two neighborhood parks, and I would like to understand how their ponds might influence storm runoff. Could you recommend an introductory article or book chapter that explains how researchers evaluate these systems? I have studied basic ecology but have little background in engineering, so a source that defines the main technical terms would be especially helpful. Your example gave me a clearer direction for the project, and I appreciate any reading advice you can offer.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your student club is preparing an event and needs additional volunteers. Classmates may be willing to help but do not yet know what the work involves or how much time they would need to give. You are coordinating the invitation.\n\nWrite an email to your classmates. In your email:\n- Describe the event and when help is needed.\n- Explain the volunteers’ duties and any preparation provided.\n- Tell classmates how to sign up and what information to include.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your classmates\nSubject: Volunteers needed for our club event', ["volunteers", "event", "sign"],
     "Hi everyone,\nOur science club is organizing a hands-on activity afternoon for local school students next Saturday, and we need four more volunteers. The event runs from one to four in the community hall. Volunteers will help small groups follow experiment instructions, keep materials organized, and guide visitors between tables. You do not need advanced science knowledge because we will provide a short training session before the doors open. If you can help, please reply by Wednesday and say whether you can stay for the full afternoon or only part of it. This will let us assign duties fairly and make sure every table has support. Thank you for considering it.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('A textbook you ordered has arrived with pages missing. You need the book for your course, so waiting without knowing what will happen is a problem. You have purchase information that will help the bookstore locate your order and resolve the issue.\n\nWrite an email to the bookstore team. In your email:\n- Describe the missing pages and identify your purchase.\n- Explain why a timely solution matters.\n- Request a replacement or another suitable remedy.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the bookstore team\nSubject: Incomplete textbook order', ["textbook", "pages", "replacement"],
     "Dear Bookstore Team,\nI received my copy of Introduction to Environmental Studies yesterday, but pages 81 through 96 are missing. The binding appears intact, so this seems to be a printing problem rather than damage during delivery. I ordered the book online on Monday under order number 4182, and I have attached photographs of the gap and my receipt. Could you arrange a replacement copy? Our class will use the missing chapter next week, so collecting a checked copy from the campus shop would be faster than another delivery. Please also let me know how to return the incomplete book without an additional charge. Thank you for resolving this.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You are considering possible research projects and would like to understand the work of a university laboratory before choosing. You want to ask the supervisor whether a short observation visit would be possible without interfering with the team’s research or safety procedures.\n\nWrite an email to the laboratory supervisor. In your email:\n- Explain your interests and why this laboratory is relevant.\n- Ask for a visit and say what you hope to learn.\n- Offer possible times and acknowledge the lab’s practical restrictions.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the laboratory supervisor\nSubject: Request to observe a lab session', ["lab", "project", "visit"],
     "Dear Professor Lee,\nI am a second-year biology student considering a project on plant responses to dry conditions. Your laboratory's work on roots and soil communities seems closely related to my interests. Would it be possible to observe a routine lab session before I choose my project topic? I would like to understand the methods students use and the preparation a beginner would need. I am free on Tuesday morning or Thursday after two, but I can adjust to a time that is less disruptive for your team. I would follow all safety instructions and understand that some activities may not be open to visitors. Thank you for considering my request.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You have noticed that lights in your residence building remain on at full brightness even when there is substantial daylight. You think a small adjustment might save energy, but you know that safe access must be maintained for everyone using the building.\n\nWrite an email to the housing manager. In your email:\n- Describe the location and your observation.\n- Suggest a practical adjustment or trial.\n- Explain how your proposal would preserve safety.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the housing manager\nSubject: A possible lighting improvement', ["lights", "housing", "safety"],
     "Dear Housing Manager,\nI have noticed that the lights in our residence building's glass entrance lobby remain at full brightness throughout sunny afternoons. The space already receives substantial daylight, so this may be using more electricity than necessary. Could the building team check whether the lighting schedule or daylight sensor can be adjusted? I am not suggesting switching off the lights in the stairwells, where steady illumination is important for safety. A trial in the entrance lobby would allow staff to see whether lower daytime brightness still provides comfortable visibility. I would be happy to help collect residents' feedback during the trial. Thank you for considering a small improvement to the building.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your study group has arranged a meeting, but you can no longer attend. You have already completed part of the work the group expects from you. You want your absence to cause as little difficulty as possible and to stay informed about decisions.\n\nWrite an email to your study group. In your email:\n- Explain why you cannot attend.\n- Share or explain your completed contribution.\n- Suggest how you can catch up and handle any follow-up work.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your study group\nSubject: My contribution to the next meeting', ["meeting", "notes", "group"],
     "Hi everyone,\nI am sorry that I cannot attend our study-group meeting tomorrow evening because my required lab session has been extended. I have finished summarizing the chapter on sampling methods and attached my notes, including two practice questions for us to discuss. Please use them during the meeting and add comments where my explanation needs clarification. Could someone send me the group's answers and any decisions about next week's revision plan afterward? I can also join a brief online call on Friday to review points I missed. I do not want my absence to slow the group down, so please continue with the original meeting time. Thanks for your understanding.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('Your department is reviewing its orientation program and has asked students for suggestions. Thinking about your own experience, you have identified a change that could help new students. The coordinator wants an idea that can be put into practice with available resources.\n\nWrite an email to the orientation coordinator. In your email:\n- Describe one specific change.\n- Explain the problem it would solve for new students.\n- Suggest a practical way to introduce or test it.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the orientation coordinator\nSubject: A suggestion for new-student orientation', ["orientation", "students", "tour"],
     "Dear Orientation Coordinator,\nI would like to suggest adding a short practical campus tour to next year's orientation. The presentations this year explained many services, but several new students, including me, still struggled to find places such as the media desk and accessibility office. Small groups led by current students could visit four essential locations immediately after the main presentation. Each stop would include one example of when a student might use the service. This could fit into thirty minutes and use volunteer guides already helping with orientation. A simple route map would let late arrivals follow the same tour independently. I think this change would turn useful information into something students can act on.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You are preparing a class project that would benefit from photographs of objects in a museum. You do not know whether the museum permits photography or allows students to include images in coursework. You want permission before making plans for your visit.\n\nWrite an email to the museum staff. In your email:\n- Describe your project and the objects you hope to photograph.\n- Explain how the photographs would be used.\n- Ask about permission and any restrictions you should follow.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the museum staff\nSubject: Photography permission for a class project', ["photographs", "museum", "permission"],
     "Dear Museum Education Team,\nI am preparing a class presentation about how everyday objects reveal local history, and I would like to photograph three household items in your permanent gallery. The images would appear only in a presentation for my seminar and would not be posted publicly or used commercially. Could you confirm whether this use is permitted and whether particular objects have additional restrictions? I would take photographs without flash and avoid including other visitors. If original photography is not allowed, I would be grateful for approved images or a link to your educational collection. I will credit the museum and include any required object information. Thank you for your guidance.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('A volunteer shift has fewer people available than expected. You are concerned that the remaining volunteers may not be able to complete the work safely and efficiently. You want to alert the coordinator and help find a temporary solution rather than simply complain.\n\nWrite an email to the volunteer coordinator. In your email:\n- Explain the staffing difficulty and its likely effect.\n- Suggest a realistic temporary solution.\n- Say what help you can personally offer.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the volunteer coordinator\nSubject: Staffing for the upcoming shift', ["volunteers", "shift", "training"],
     "Dear Volunteer Coordinator,\nDuring yesterday's afternoon shift, only two volunteers were available to organize donations and help visitors at the same time. We managed the essential work, but several people waited much longer than usual. Could we invite volunteers from the morning team to cover a one-hour overlap next week? That would help during the busiest period without requiring everyone to take a full extra shift. I can prepare a short checklist of the tasks that need immediate attention and help train anyone who is unfamiliar with the afternoon routine. Please let me know whether the overlap is possible or whether another arrangement would work better. Thank you for coordinating our efforts.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You found a notebook in a seminar room after class. You want to contact the owner through the class mailing list without sharing private contents or making it easy for someone else to claim the notebook. You can keep it safe temporarily.\n\nWrite an email to your class. In your email:\n- Describe enough features for the owner to recognize the notebook.\n- Explain how the owner can confirm that it belongs to them.\n- Arrange a safe way to return it.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your class\nSubject: Notebook found after the seminar', ["notebook", "seminar", "collect"],
     "Hi everyone,\nI found a blue notebook under a seat near the window in Room 23 after our history seminar this morning. It has an elastic strap and a small white sticker on the front. I have not read the notes inside, but it seems likely to belong to someone in our class. If it is yours, please reply with a brief description of the sticker so I can make sure it reaches the right person. I will leave it with the department reception desk tomorrow afternoon if nobody contacts me before then. You can also collect it from me after Wednesday's seminar. I hope this helps reunite the owner with their work.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('Your course includes a field activity, but one part of the planned route or procedure presents an accessibility barrier for you. You want to participate in the learning rather than simply be excused. Your instructor may be able to arrange an alternative.\n\nWrite an email to your instructor. In your email:\n- Explain the relevant barrier without unnecessary personal detail.\n- Request an alternative arrangement.\n- Explain how that arrangement could preserve the activity’s learning goal.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your instructor\nSubject: An accessible arrangement for the field activity', ["field", "accessible", "observation"],
     "Dear Professor Rivera,\nI am writing about next week's river observation activity. The route description includes a long flight of uneven steps, which I cannot safely use. I would still like to complete the same learning objectives and contribute observations to my group. Is there an accessible section of the river where I could record flow conditions and land use? If necessary, I could work with published photographs for the part of the route I cannot reach and compare them with my partners' notes. Please let me know which arrangement would meet the assignment requirements. I am available to discuss the practical details before class. Thank you for helping me participate fully.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You are organizing a student panel and would like to invite a local researcher whose work relates to the topic. The researcher will need to understand the audience, the expected contribution, and the time commitment before deciding whether to take part.\n\nWrite an email to the researcher. In your email:\n- Describe the panel topic and its audience.\n- Explain the contribution and time commitment you are requesting.\n- Invite a reply and offer to provide further details.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the researcher\nSubject: Invitation to a student panel', ["panel", "students", "research"],
     "Dear Dr. Morgan,\nOur student research society is holding a panel on how local questions can become useful research projects, and we would be delighted if you could participate. The audience will mainly be undergraduates beginning their first independent studies. We hope you could give a ten-minute account of your neighborhood transport project and then join a twenty-minute discussion with two student presenters. The event is planned for October 18 at four in the afternoon, with an online connection available if travel is inconvenient. Please let us know whether you would be interested and if the proposed time works for you. We can send a more detailed program before you decide.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('While editing a shared project document, you accidentally replaced some of the group’s work. You have begun checking whether earlier versions can be recovered. You want to tell your teammates promptly, take responsibility, and avoid a similar problem during future edits.\n\nWrite an email to your project group. In your email:\n- Explain what happened and which work may be affected.\n- Describe the recovery steps you have taken or propose to take.\n- Suggest a practical way to prevent another accidental overwrite.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your project group\nSubject: Recovering our shared document', ["document", "backup", "version"],
     "Hi everyone,\nI accidentally replaced the methods section and the opening paragraph of the results while uploading my revised section this afternoon. I am sorry for the confusion. I have already checked the version history and restored the most recent complete draft, but please review your own section to confirm that no final changes are missing. My new text is now in a separate file so we can compare it carefully before merging. To prevent this happening again, I suggest editing our assigned sections in the shared document instead of uploading whole replacement files. We could also save a dated backup before major revisions. Please tell me if anything still needs recovering, and I will help fix it.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('Some members of your student club commute and have difficulty attending the current meeting time. Other members have schedules that also need to be considered. You want to propose a fair change to the leader rather than assume one arrangement suits everyone.\n\nWrite an email to the club leader. In your email:\n- Explain the difficulty faced by commuters.\n- Propose a specific change to the schedule.\n- Address a concern that other members might have.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the club leader\nSubject: Meeting times for commuting members', ["commuters", "meeting", "schedule"],
     "Dear Club Leader,\nSeveral commuter members have mentioned that our eight o'clock evening meetings finish after the last convenient train. I enjoy participating, but leaving early means missing decisions and volunteer assignments. Could we try meeting at six on alternate weeks? This would let commuters stay for the full discussion while preserving some later meetings for members with afternoon classes. We could also circulate the agenda beforehand and keep routine announcements in a written update, making the meeting shorter. A brief survey after a month would show whether the trial improves attendance without creating new difficulties. Thank you for considering a schedule that includes more of the club's members.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('After submitting an application, you noticed that one detail was incorrect. You do not want to submit a second application and create confusion. You have the application information and the correct detail available so the office can advise you.\n\nWrite an email to the application office. In your email:\n- Identify your application and the incorrect detail.\n- Provide the correct information.\n- Ask what steps are needed to update the application.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the application office\nSubject: Correction to a submitted application', ["application", "correction", "date"],
     "Dear Applications Office,\nI submitted my application for the summer field course yesterday and noticed that I entered an incorrect availability date. The application is under the name Alex Chen, with reference FC318. I listed June 12 as my earliest start date, but I am actually available from June 19 because my examinations finish the previous day. All other information remains correct. Could you update the date, or should I complete a correction form through the portal? I understand that availability affects group allocation and wanted to notify you before placements are assigned. Please let me know if you need any supporting information. Thank you for your help correcting the record.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('A local organization provided information or assistance for your class project. You have now completed the work and want the organization to know that its contribution was useful. You would also like to share the result without assuming the staff want a large attachment.\n\nWrite an email to the organization’s staff. In your email:\n- Thank the organization and explain how its help was used.\n- Share a specific project outcome.\n- Offer a copy of the report or another appropriate follow-up.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the organization’s staff\nSubject: Thank you for supporting our project', ["project", "report", "thank"],
     "Dear Community Center Team,\nThank you for allowing our class to interview volunteers about the neighborhood reading program. Your explanations helped us understand why flexible meeting times matter for families, something we had overlooked when designing our initial survey. We used that insight to revise our questions and received much clearer responses. Our final report recommends preserving the evening sessions while providing a simple reminder system for participants. We would be pleased to send you a copy if it would be useful for future planning. The report contains no names or identifying quotations from the interviews. We appreciate the time you gave us and the opportunity to learn from your practical experience.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('A delivery left at your apartment is addressed to someone in another apartment. You have kept the package unopened and want the delivery service to arrange the correction. You cannot remain at home all day waiting for someone to collect it.\n\nWrite an email to the delivery service. In your email:\n- Describe the delivery error and useful identifying details.\n- Request collection or another safe correction.\n- Give a suitable time or arrangement for the handover.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the delivery service\nSubject: Package delivered to the wrong apartment', ["package", "delivery", "collection"],
     "Dear Delivery Team,\nA package was left at my apartment this morning, but the label is addressed to Apartment 42 in the neighboring building. I live in Apartment 24, and I have kept the package unopened. The tracking number on the label is 762184. Could you arrange to collect it and deliver it to the correct address? I will be home tomorrow between nine and noon, or I can leave it with our staffed reception desk if that is easier for the driver. Please confirm the collection arrangement before I hand it over. I would like the intended recipient to receive it promptly without risking another mistaken delivery. Thank you for your assistance.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You have prepared an early outline for an essay and would like advice before developing the full draft. Your instructor cannot review every detail at this stage, so you want to identify the part of your argument that most needs clarification.\n\nWrite an email to your instructor. In your email:\n- Summarize your intended argument.\n- Identify a specific uncertainty in the outline.\n- Request focused advice that would help you continue drafting.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your instructor\nSubject: Feedback on an essay outline', ["outline", "argument", "feedback"],
     "Dear Professor Adams,\nI have attached an outline for my essay on the preservation of everyday printed materials. My main argument is that surviving library collections do not fully represent what people read in the past, because inexpensive items were often discarded. I plan to compare library catalogs with publishers' records and examples of paper reused in book bindings. My uncertainty is whether this comparison is too broad for the assignment length. Could you advise whether I should focus on one city or one type of printed material? I am seeking guidance on the scope rather than a detailed edit at this stage. Thank you for helping me develop a manageable and well-supported argument.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('The campus café has invited suggestions from its customers. You have noticed a problem that a small change could address without redesigning the whole service. You want to explain who would benefit and propose a manageable way to test your idea.\n\nWrite an email to the café manager. In your email:\n- Describe your suggested improvement.\n- Explain which customers would benefit and why.\n- Suggest a limited trial and a way to assess it.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the café manager\nSubject: A small improvement to the café service', ["café", "menu", "trial"],
     "Dear Café Manager,\nI would like to suggest displaying a clear ingredient list for the prepared sandwiches beside the menu. Students currently need to ask staff about each item, which can slow the lunch queue and make it difficult for people managing dietary restrictions. A short printed list would answer common questions while still directing customers with serious allergies to staff for confirmation. You could trial the display for two weeks using the three most popular sandwiches, then ask employees whether it reduces repeated questions. This would not require changing the food itself, and updates could be made whenever recipes change. Thank you for considering a practical improvement to the ordering experience.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your study partner has missed several sessions you arranged together. This has affected your preparation, but you do not know why the sessions were missed. You want to discuss a workable arrangement while keeping the message respectful and open to an explanation.\n\nWrite an email to your study partner. In your email:\n- Describe the effect of the missed sessions.\n- Ask about your partner’s situation without making assumptions.\n- Propose a new arrangement that you can both discuss.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your study partner\nSubject: Planning our study sessions', ["sessions", "schedule", "study"],
     "Hi Jordan,\nI wanted to check whether our current study schedule still works for you. We missed the last two Tuesday sessions, and I waited at the library because I did not know your plans had changed. I understand that coursework can become unexpectedly busy. Could you let me know whether a different day or a shorter session would be easier? I would be happy to meet online for forty minutes on Thursday instead. It would also help if we both sent a quick confirmation on the morning of each meeting. I value studying together and would like an arrangement that is realistic for both of us. Let me know what you think.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('Your department would like to keep a student exhibition open longer than planned, but some of its materials are borrowed. The owner may need them back on the agreed date. You want permission for an extension and are prepared to use an alternative.\n\nWrite an email to the owner of the exhibition materials. In your email:\n- Explain the reason for the extension.\n- Propose a new return date and explain how the materials will be cared for.\n- Offer an alternative if the originals must be returned as agreed.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the owner of the exhibition materials\nSubject: Request to extend an exhibition loan', ["exhibition", "materials", "return"],
     "Dear Ms. Patel,\nThank you again for lending the historical maps to our student exhibition. The department has asked whether the display could remain open for visiting school groups next week, so I am writing to request a short extension of the loan. We would return the maps on Friday, October 23, rather than this Friday. They would remain in the same locked display case under the agreed lighting conditions. If you need the originals back on the original date, we can remove them and use labeled reproductions for the extra week instead. Please let me know which arrangement you prefer. We appreciate your generosity and will respect the return date that works for you.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You missed a scheduled class presentation because you were ill. Your presentation materials are ready, and you would like to complete the work responsibly. You need to ask the instructor about another opportunity without assuming that the original assessment requirements will be waived.\n\nWrite an email to your instructor. In your email:\n- Briefly explain your absence.\n- Propose a time or arrangement for presenting.\n- Ask how to meet the same requirements as the other students.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your instructor\nSubject: Request for a make-up presentation', ["presentation", "illness", "requirements"],
     "Dear Professor Gomez,\nI was unable to attend yesterday's seminar because I was ill, and I am sorry to have missed my scheduled presentation. I have completed the slides and supporting notes and would like to arrange a make-up opportunity. Could I present at the beginning of next Tuesday's class, or during an office-hour appointment if class time is limited? I would also like to know how to complete the question-and-answer component so that I meet the same requirements as the other students. I can send the slides now and provide the absence documentation requested by the department. Thank you for considering an arrangement that lets me complete the work responsibly.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your project group is preparing a survey for other students. One draft question combines ideas that respondents might evaluate differently, so a single answer may be hard to interpret. You want to raise the concern before the survey is distributed.\n\nWrite an email to your project team. In your email:\n- Identify the unclear question and explain the problem.\n- Suggest a clearer revision.\n- Propose a small check of the new wording before distribution.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your project team\nSubject: A revision to our survey question', ["survey", "question", "test"],
     "Hi team,\nI think we should revise the survey question that asks whether students find campus transport cheap and reliable. Those are two separate issues, so someone might agree about the price but disagree about punctuality. A single answer would be difficult to interpret. Could we split it into two questions, one about affordability and another about whether services arrive when expected? We could then test the revised wording with three classmates before sending the survey more widely. This small check should reveal whether the response options are clear without delaying our schedule. I have added a suggested version to the shared draft and would appreciate your comments before tomorrow afternoon.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('You registered for a seminar, but the confirmation message lists a different session from the one you selected. The listed session conflicts with another commitment. You want the organizer to check your registration and help you find a workable arrangement.\n\nWrite an email to the seminar organizer. In your email:\n- Identify your registration and clarify the session you selected.\n- Explain the scheduling constraint.\n- Request a correction and ask about an alternative if needed.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the seminar organizer\nSubject: Incorrect session in my confirmation', ["registration", "session", "confirmation"],
     "Dear Seminar Organizer,\nMy registration confirmation lists the afternoon session on research ethics, but I selected the morning session when completing the form. My confirmation number is RE204, and the registration was submitted on Monday. I have a required class beginning at two, so attending the afternoon session would mean missing part of the discussion. Could you check whether the morning booking can be restored? If it is now full, please place me on its waiting list and let me know whether an equivalent session is planned later in the month. I am keen to complete the training before starting my project. Thank you for helping resolve the scheduling mismatch.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Several students would like to run a short peer workshop on a skill useful for their coursework. You are willing to help organize it, but you need departmental permission to use a suitable room. The department will want to know how the session will be managed.\n\nWrite an email to the department coordinator. In your email:\n- Outline the topic and intended participants.\n- Request an appropriate space and time.\n- Explain how you will organize the session responsibly.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the department coordinator\nSubject: Room request for a peer workshop', ["workshop", "room", "students"],
     "Dear Department Coordinator,\nSeveral students in our class would like to hold a peer workshop on organizing references for research papers. We have experience with the library's recommended software and could demonstrate a simple workflow before helping participants practice with their own sources. May we use a small computer room for one hour next Wednesday afternoon? We would limit attendance to twelve students, circulate preparation instructions beforehand, and leave the room ready for the next class. The workshop would supplement the library's formal training rather than replace it, and we would direct technical questions to library staff. Please let me know whether a suitable room is available and if we need to complete a booking form.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('While reviewing a class handout, you notice a possible error in one example. You may have misunderstood the explanation, so you want to ask rather than simply announce that the material is wrong. The example matters for an assignment you are preparing.\n\nWrite an email to your instructor. In your email:\n- Identify the example and the possible error.\n- Explain the reasoning behind your concern.\n- Ask for clarification or a correction so you can continue your work.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your instructor\nSubject: Clarification about an example in the handout', ["handout", "figure", "clarification"],
     "Dear Professor Singh,\nWhile reviewing the sampling handout, I noticed that the percentages in the table on page three add up to 110 rather than 100. I may be misunderstanding the categories, but the text appears to describe them as mutually exclusive groups. Could you clarify whether one figure contains a typing error or whether respondents were allowed to select more than one category? I am using this example to prepare for the problem set and want to apply the method correctly. If a correction is needed, a short note on the course page might also help classmates who have begun their revision. Thank you for checking the example and explaining the intended interpretation.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your local library has invited ideas for community events. You have an activity in mind that would interest a particular audience and could use the library’s resources. The events team needs enough information to judge whether the proposal is practical.\n\nWrite an email to the library events team. In your email:\n- Identify the intended audience and the purpose of the event.\n- Describe what participants would do.\n- Explain the resources and organizational help needed.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the library events team\nSubject: Proposal for a community event', ["library", "event", "maps"],
     "Dear Library Events Team,\nI would like to propose a small event called Reading Our Neighborhood Through Old Maps. It would be suitable for teenagers and adults interested in local history, with no prior knowledge required. Participants could compare copies of maps from different decades and discuss how roads, public spaces, and place names changed. The library would need a meeting room, printed map copies, and a projector for the introduction. I could help prepare a simple worksheet and guide the discussion alongside a staff member. Using reproductions would protect the originals while allowing people to annotate their observations. Please let me know whether this idea fits your community program and how I could develop it further.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Repair work in your residence building has been noisy while you are preparing for an online examination. You know the work may be necessary, but you need to plan a period of uninterrupted practice. The housing office may be able to suggest an arrangement.\n\nWrite an email to the housing office. In your email:\n- Describe the noise and its conflict with your practice.\n- Ask for the planned repair schedule.\n- Request a practical option that would allow you to prepare.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the housing office\nSubject: Repair schedule and examination practice', ["noise", "repair", "schedule"],
     "Dear Housing Office,\nRepair work outside my residence room has been producing loud drilling noise during the afternoon. I understand that the work is necessary, but I have a scheduled online practice assessment on Thursday from two to three and need to hear spoken prompts clearly. Could you tell me whether drilling is planned during that period? If it cannot be paused, is there a quiet room elsewhere in the residence complex that I could reserve for an hour? I can bring my own laptop and headphones, so I only need a desk and a stable internet connection. Advance information would let me prepare without interfering with the repair team's work. Thank you for your help.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You are doing a student project and believe a local organization may already collect useful information. You do not need personal records, and you want to avoid creating unnecessary work for its staff. You would like to request suitable data respectfully.\n\nWrite an email to the local organization. In your email:\n- Explain your project and the information you need.\n- Specify that the request does not require personal identifying information.\n- Offer to acknowledge the organization and share useful findings.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the local organization\nSubject: Request for non-identifying project data', ["data", "project", "visitors"],
     "Dear Community Garden Coordinator,\nI am completing a course project on how use of public spaces changes across the year. Would your organization be willing to share monthly totals of garden visits from the past year, if those figures are already available? I do not need names, contact information, or individual attendance records. The project would compare broad seasonal patterns and discuss how opening hours may affect participation. I would clearly acknowledge the source and any limitations you identify, such as changes in counting methods. After the course, I would be happy to send a short summary of the findings for your own planning. Please let me know whether this request is feasible without creating extra work for your team.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You would like to review course material with a classmate before an upcoming quiz. A focused session could help both of you identify gaps, but you have not yet agreed on a plan. You want to suggest a practical arrangement and preparation.\n\nWrite an email to your classmate. In your email:\n- Explain the purpose of the session.\n- Propose a time and place while allowing for your classmate’s schedule.\n- Describe what each of you should prepare.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your classmate\nSubject: Invitation to a study session', ["study", "session", "questions"],
     "Hi Morgan,\nWould you like to review the ecology material together before next week's quiz? I suggest meeting in the library collaboration area on Friday at three for about an hour. We could each prepare three questions that require explanations rather than simple definitions, then take turns answering without looking at our notes. Afterward, we could check the textbook and discuss any gaps. I can focus on plant-soil interactions if you would like to prepare questions about pollination networks. This should keep the session focused and give us both a chance to practice retrieval. If Friday does not work, let me know another time over the weekend that suits you.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('You registered for a conference and noticed that the fee appears twice in your payment record. You intended to register only once. Registration support will need enough information to locate both charges without confusing your request with a cancellation.\n\nWrite an email to conference registration support. In your email:\n- Describe the two charges and identify your registration.\n- Provide useful payment details.\n- Request correction while making clear whether you still plan to attend.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: conference registration support\nSubject: Duplicate conference fee', ["fee", "payment", "refund"],
     "Dear Registration Support,\nI registered for the undergraduate research conference yesterday and appear to have been charged the registration fee twice. Both charges are for thirty dollars and were recorded within a few minutes of each other. My registration reference is UR619, and I received only one confirmation email. Could you check the payment record and refund the duplicate charge if it has been processed? I have attached a receipt showing the transaction dates and amounts, with unrelated account information removed. Please keep my conference place active, since I still intend to attend and present my poster. Let me know if you need another reference to locate the payments. Thank you for resolving the duplicate transaction.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Visitors often seem confused at a particular point inside a campus building. You have noticed that the signs do not clearly show where an important service or room is located. You want facilities staff to consider a specific improvement to the directions.\n\nWrite an email to the facilities team. In your email:\n- Identify the confusing location.\n- Explain who is affected and what goes wrong.\n- Propose clear, appropriately placed signs.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the facilities team\nSubject: Clearer signs inside the building', ["signs", "building", "entrance"],
     "Dear Facilities Team,\nVisitors to the language center often enter through the main doors and then struggle to find the reception desk, which is around the corner on the lower level. I have helped several new students who assumed the building was unstaffed because the first corridor looked empty. Could you install a simple directional sign opposite the entrance and another beside the lift? Including the accessible route would help visitors who cannot use the stairs. A temporary printed sign could test whether the wording and position work before permanent signs are ordered. Clearer directions would save staff repeated interruptions and make the building easier to use. Thank you for considering this suggestion.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your group project has reached a stage where the existing division of work no longer seems balanced. You want to discuss a change before the deadline becomes a problem. Your teammates may have different schedules and strengths, so agreement is important.\n\nWrite an email to your teammates. In your email:\n- Explain your concern about the current arrangement.\n- Propose a fair distribution of specific tasks.\n- Invite teammates to discuss or adjust the proposal.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your teammates\nSubject: Reviewing our division of work', ["group", "tasks", "analysis"],
     "Hi team,\nAfter reviewing our project plan, I think the data-analysis section may require considerably more time than the introduction and formatting tasks. At present, one person is responsible for all the analysis, which could create a bottleneck near the deadline. Could we divide it into data checking and interpretation, with two people working together? I can take responsibility for checking the spreadsheet and documenting any corrections, while another member drafts the explanation of the findings. We could then review the introduction and final formatting as a group. Please share your availability before we agree on changes so the workload remains fair. I hope this adjustment will make our final week more manageable.\nBest,\nAlex"),
    ('A mentor recently gave you advice before an interview. You have now had an opportunity to use that advice and want to explain how it helped. You would also like to keep the mentor informed about what you plan to do next.\n\nWrite an email to your mentor. In your email:\n- Thank the mentor and identify a useful piece of advice.\n- Describe how you applied it and the result.\n- Mention an appropriate next step.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your mentor\nSubject: Thank you for the interview advice', ["interview", "advice", "example"],
     "Dear Ms. Brown,\nThank you for the practical advice you gave me during last week's interview workshop. Your suggestion to prepare one specific example for each major skill helped me organize my answers much more clearly. In yesterday's internship interview, I described how our project group recovered from a missed deadline instead of simply saying that I work well with others. The interviewer asked a useful follow-up question, and I felt better able to explain my own contribution. I still need to make my opening answers shorter, so I plan to practice with a friend and time each response. I appreciate your guidance and wanted you to know how directly it helped my preparation.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('An educational video assigned in your course is difficult for you to follow without captions. You want to complete the learning task, not avoid it. The course team may be able to add captions or provide another resource that preserves the important content.\n\nWrite an email to the course team. In your email:\n- Explain the difficulty you are experiencing.\n- Request captions or other appropriate support.\n- Suggest a useful alternative if captions are not immediately available.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the course team\nSubject: Support for the assigned video', ["video", "captions", "transcript"],
     "Dear Course Team,\nThe recording of Monday's guest lecture is very useful, but several sections are difficult to follow because the microphone is faint and technical terms are unfamiliar. Could captions or a checked transcript be added to the video? This would help students review the terminology accurately and make the material easier to access in different listening conditions. If a full transcript takes time, sharing the speaker's slides and a list of key terms would be a helpful temporary alternative. I can identify the timestamps where the sound is especially unclear if that assists the process. Thank you for making the lecture available and for considering ways to improve its usability.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('Your class would benefit from seeing how work is carried out in a local organization. You would like to ask the manager about a short visit, but you understand that visitors could interrupt normal operations. Your proposal should be limited and practical.\n\nWrite an email to the workplace manager. In your email:\n- Explain the learning purpose of the visit.\n- Propose a manageable visit and provide useful details.\n- Acknowledge operational constraints and offer flexibility.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: the workplace manager\nSubject: Request for a short class visit', ["visit", "students", "workplace"],
     "Dear Workshop Manager,\nOur materials class is studying how repair decisions can extend the useful life of everyday objects. Would it be possible for a small group of six students to visit your bicycle workshop for a brief explanation of how staff assess damaged parts? We are interested in the decision process rather than observing customers or interrupting repairs. A twenty-minute visit before opening or during another quiet period would be sufficient. We would follow all safety instructions and would not take photographs without permission. If a visit is impractical, a short online conversation would also be valuable. Please let us know whether either option might fit your schedule. Thank you for considering our request.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
    ('You have discovered that two courses required for your program overlap in the timetable. You need both courses to make progress, but you cannot attend both as currently scheduled. Your adviser may know about alternative sections or other acceptable arrangements.\n\nWrite an email to your academic adviser. In your email:\n- Identify the courses and the timetable conflict.\n- Explain its consequence for your study plan.\n- Ask about alternatives or the next steps you should take.\n\nWrite in complete sentences.\nTo: your academic adviser\nSubject: Overlap between required courses', ["courses", "timetable", "alternative"],
     "Dear Academic Adviser,\nThe new timetable places the required statistics tutorial and the environmental methods laboratory at the same time on Thursday mornings. Both courses are listed as necessary for my program this semester, and missing either regularly would prevent me from completing the practical work. Could you advise whether another tutorial group is available or whether the department plans to adjust the schedule? I checked the registration portal, but the only alternative statistics group currently appears full. I would prefer to resolve the overlap before classes begin rather than alternate absences. Please let me know if I should join a waiting list or contact either course coordinator directly. Thank you for helping me plan a workable schedule.\nBest regards,\nAlex"),
]

DISCUSSIONS = [
    ('Students sometimes propose small research projects that fall outside their regular coursework. A university is considering a fund for these projects and must decide whether supporting beginners is a good use of a limited research budget. Should universities offer small grants for student-led research?', 'Small grants let students test original ideas. Trying a modest project would teach students how to turn a question into evidence. Even an unsuccessful attempt could reveal practical limits that are difficult to understand from reading about completed research.', 'Funding should go to established researchers. Experienced researchers are more likely to have the equipment and methods needed to produce useful results. Dividing a small budget among many beginners could leave serious ongoing projects without essential support.', ["research", "students", "funding"],
     "I support small grants for student-led research, provided that students receive basic supervision. The first student is right that even modest funding can turn an idea into a practical investigation. For example, a group studying campus travel could use a small grant to print accessible surveys and count traffic at several locations. The educational value comes from planning the study, managing resources, and responding to imperfect evidence. Established researchers still need substantial support, but a limited student fund would not have to compete with major laboratory projects. Requiring a short public report would also make the results useful to the campus and encourage students to explain both findings and limitations."),
    ('A city is considering restricting cars on streets immediately beside schools. The proposal aims to change conditions when children arrive and leave, but the same streets are also used by residents, deliveries, and people traveling elsewhere. Should cities introduce car-free streets near schools?', 'They would make walking safer. Children would have fewer moving vehicles to negotiate at the school gate. Parents might also feel more comfortable letting older children walk, rather than adding another car to the congestion.', 'They could inconvenience nearby residents. A restriction could move traffic to neighboring streets rather than remove it. Residents who need to reach their homes or receive deliveries should not find ordinary journeys much harder because of where they live.', ["streets", "schools", "residents"],
     "Cities should trial car-free streets near schools during arrival and departure times. Safer walking conditions are a strong benefit, but the concern about residents deserves a practical response. A limited schedule would reduce disruption compared with closing a street all day. The city could provide clear access arrangements for residents with mobility needs and monitor whether traffic simply shifts to another unsafe location. For example, a school could begin with one term and collect observations from families and neighbors. If the trial reduces conflicts between vehicles and pedestrians without creating serious access problems, the policy could become permanent. Evaluation should guide expansion rather than assuming the same arrangement suits every street."),
    ('An instructor is deciding whether students may revise marked assignments for additional credit. Revision could make feedback more useful, but it would also change how students plan their first submissions and how often work must be assessed. Should students be allowed to revise assignments after feedback?', 'Revision encourages learning from mistakes. Comments become much more meaningful when a student has to apply them to the same piece of work. Otherwise, it is easy to look only at the grade and repeat the mistake next time.', "It could increase instructors' workload. Reading the same assignment twice takes time away from planning lessons and helping the class. Students who need many rounds of revision could consume much more attention than those who submit careful work initially.", ["revision", "feedback", "learning"],
     "Students should have a limited opportunity to revise assignments because using feedback is itself an important academic skill. A comment has little value if students read the grade and never apply the advice. However, unlimited resubmissions would create the workload problem mentioned by the second student. I would allow one revision of a major assignment and require a short explanation of the changes. Instructors could then focus on whether students addressed the central issues rather than marking every sentence again. For example, revising an essay's evidence and organization would demonstrate deeper learning than merely correcting punctuation. This approach connects assessment with improvement while keeping the process manageable for teachers and students."),
    ('Public libraries increasingly consider services beyond traditional book lending. One town has proposed a collection of household tools that residents could borrow for occasional tasks, using space and staff time that must be shared with existing services. Should public libraries lend tools as well as books?', 'Borrowing tools can reduce waste and household costs. Many households buy equipment for a single job and then store it unused. A borrowing service could make those occasional tasks affordable and prevent each household from purchasing its own rarely used item.', 'Libraries may lack storage and maintenance staff. Tools need safety checks and repairs that are different from caring for books. Without suitable staff and storage, the new collection could take resources away from services the library already knows how to provide.', ["libraries", "tools", "maintenance"],
     "A small tool-lending service could be valuable if a library has the capacity to maintain it. Many people need an item such as a drill only a few times a year, so shared access can reduce both expense and unnecessary purchases. Nevertheless, the second student identifies a real constraint: lending damaged equipment would undermine the service. I would begin with a narrow selection of simple tools, clear borrowing instructions, and a partnership with a local repair organization. Demand and maintenance time should be recorded during the trial. Libraries should expand only if the service supports their community role without weakening essential book, information, and study services that residents already depend on."),
    ('A university department wants assessments to reflect the kinds of work graduates will do with other people. It is considering more group projects, while students have raised questions about whether a shared mark represents individual learning fairly. Should university courses use more group assessments?', 'Group work develops collaboration. Working with others involves explaining ideas, negotiating priorities, and responding when a plan changes. Those abilities do not develop fully when every assignment can be completed alone without depending on anyone else.', 'Individual contributions can be hard to measure. A polished final product can hide very unequal effort. A student who did most of the work may receive the same mark as someone who contributed little, making the grade difficult to interpret.', ["group", "assessment", "contribution"],
     "Group assessment is useful when collaboration is part of the learning objective, but it should not replace all individual work. The first student correctly notes that students need practice coordinating decisions and sharing responsibility. The difficulty is ensuring that a group mark reflects meaningful participation. One solution is to combine a shared product with an individual explanation of each student's decisions and learning. For example, a research team could submit one report while members separately discuss their contribution and a challenge the team faced. This would make uneven participation easier to identify without reducing collaboration to a list of hours. Assessment should reward both the quality of the joint work and each person's understanding."),
    ('A museum must protect fragile objects while helping visitors understand them. Staff are considering adding replicas that can be handled, alongside original objects that must stay behind glass. The change would require both space and explanation. Should museums display replicas that visitors can touch?', 'Touch can make exhibits more engaging and accessible. Handling a copy can reveal shape and construction in ways a distant view cannot. It could also offer a meaningful experience to visitors who cannot easily use the museum’s usual visual displays.', 'Replicas may distract from authentic objects. Visitors may spend their time with the most entertaining activity instead of looking carefully at the historical object. A museum should help people appreciate original evidence, not make every display resemble a play area.', ["museums", "replicas", "objects"],
     "Museums should use clearly labeled touchable replicas alongside selected original objects. Physical interaction can reveal details that are difficult to understand through glass, such as the weight or shape of a tool. It can also make an exhibition more accessible to visitors who benefit from tactile information. The concern about authenticity is reasonable, but accurate labeling can distinguish evidence from interpretation. For instance, a replica pot could accompany a fragile original, with a note explaining which features are certain and which were reconstructed. The goal would be to help visitors ask better questions about the original. To limit distraction, each hands-on activity should lead visitors back to an important feature of the original."),
    ('Students in several programs want internships before graduation. One proposal is to offer placements during teaching terms rather than only during vacations, which could connect learning and work more closely but would add another weekly commitment. Should students complete internships during the academic year?', 'Internships connect coursework with practical experience. A student could encounter a problem at work and then recognize its relevance in class the next day. That immediate connection might make theories more memorable and help students identify skills they still need.', 'They can interfere with studying. A placement involves more than the hours spent at the workplace; travel and preparation also take time. Students may become too tired to engage deeply with their courses, especially near assessment deadlines.', ["internships", "study", "experience"],
     "Internships during the academic year can be worthwhile if their hours are limited and their educational purpose is clear. Practical experience may help students understand why concepts from class matter, but an internship that consumes every free afternoon can weaken the learning it is meant to support. Universities should help students choose placements with predictable schedules and a supervisor willing to discuss what they are learning. A short weekly reflection could connect workplace observations with coursework. Students with heavy laboratory or examination commitments should also have summer alternatives. I therefore favor flexible access to internships rather than a requirement that everyone participate during term, since a useful opportunity should fit a student's actual capacity."),
    ('A neighborhood has limited street space and little shade. Residents are discussing whether some parking spaces should become planting areas for trees. The proposal would affect people who walk through the area as well as people who drive there. Should neighborhoods replace some parking spaces with trees?', 'Trees improve comfort and public space. Shade could make the street more comfortable for anyone spending time outside, including people waiting for others or visiting shops. The benefit would continue beyond the brief period when one car occupies a space.', 'Parking is important for shops and residents. Some customers and residents cannot easily walk long distances from another parking area. Removing spaces without considering their needs could make local services less accessible, even if the street becomes more attractive.', ["trees", "parking", "neighborhood"],
     "Replacing selected parking spaces with trees can improve a neighborhood, but the locations should be chosen carefully. Shade may make walking more comfortable and encourage people to spend time near local shops. At the same time, removing essential loading or accessible parking spaces would create avoidable difficulties. I would begin with spaces that are rarely occupied and consult residents and businesses about practical access. The plan must also include enough soil, water, and maintenance for the trees to survive; planting alone does not guarantee a benefit. A small, well-supported project could provide evidence about street use and comfort before wider changes are made. This balances environmental improvement with everyday access needs."),
    ('A university is deciding whether every lecture should be recorded by default. Recordings could be useful after class, but automatic recording might also change attendance patterns and the way instructors and students participate during the session. Should university lectures be recorded automatically?', 'Recordings help students review difficult material. When I miss a step in an explanation, reviewing that moment helps more than guessing from incomplete notes. A recording also supports students whose health or other responsibilities occasionally prevent them from attending.', 'Students may stop attending in person. It is tempting to postpone a lecture when a recording will always be available. Students may fall behind or miss the chance to ask a question at the point when they first become confused.', ["recordings", "lectures", "students"],
     "Universities should normally record lectures while allowing exceptions for sensitive discussions or activities that involve personal information. Recordings help students revisit difficult explanations and support those who miss a session for a legitimate reason. However, a recording should supplement an engaging class, not become a reason to abandon participation. Instructors could use class time for questions and applications that add value beyond replaying information. Clear guidance about when to review the recording would also be useful. For example, students might revisit one explanation before attempting a practice problem. The attendance concern should be investigated with actual course evidence rather than used to deny a resource that can make learning more accessible and flexible."),
    ('Residents have proposed a regular event where volunteers help people repair household items. The town could provide a venue and publicity, but it must consider how this activity would relate to local businesses that charge for repair services. Should towns support repair cafés?', 'Repair cafés reduce waste and teach skills. People can learn why an item stopped working rather than immediately replacing it. Seeing a repair may also help them care for other possessions, so the benefit goes beyond saving one damaged object.', 'They may compete with paid repair businesses. Local repair businesses pay for premises, equipment, and skilled labor. A publicly supported free service could draw away customers whose payments help those businesses stay available for more complicated work.', ["repair", "skills", "businesses"],
     "Towns should support repair cafés as learning events with a clear scope. A volunteer who helps someone mend a simple household item can teach a skill and prevent unnecessary disposal. This does not need to replace professional repair services. Organizers could refer complex or safety-critical work to qualified businesses and invite those businesses to explain when expert help is necessary. For example, an event might focus on clothing repairs and basic bicycle adjustments rather than electrical faults. Recording the kinds of items brought in would help organizers understand demand. The strongest model would connect volunteers, residents, and professional repairers, creating a local culture of maintenance instead of presenting free repair as a substitute for all paid expertise."),
    ('Students encounter many online sources when preparing university assignments. A department is considering introductory lessons on checking claims and tracing evidence, but some instructors think these skills should already be in place before students enter university. Should first-year courses include lessons on evaluating online sources?', 'Students need help judging information quality. Knowing how to find a page is different from judging the evidence behind it. A student may be comfortable online yet still confuse an attractive summary with a reliable account of a study.', 'Such skills should already have been taught at school. Repeating material from school could reduce time for the actual subject. Students should arrive ready to evaluate basic information, rather than expect every university course to teach general study skills again.', ["sources", "students", "evidence"],
     "First-year courses should include source evaluation because university assignments often demand more than the skills students used at school. Knowing that a website looks professional is different from checking whether its claims are supported by appropriate evidence. Instruction should be integrated into a real assignment rather than delivered as a disconnected warning about unreliable information. For instance, students could compare an original study with a news summary and identify what changed between them. Even students with previous training would benefit from applying it to a new discipline. A short, practical exercise can establish shared expectations and help instructors see where further support is needed, without assuming that every student begins with the same preparation."),
    ('A university is examining ways to make travel to campus easier. One option is to provide public transport passes without a separate charge to students, funding the arrangement from a budget that also supports other student services. Should universities provide free public transport passes?', 'Passes improve access and reduce car use. A pass would remove the need to weigh the cost of every trip. Students could attend a short meeting or visit the library without paying another fare, making campus opportunities easier to use.', 'The cost may be too high. Students living near campus or far from transit routes may gain little from the program. Paying for everyone could leave less money for services that reach a wider range of students.', ["transport", "passes", "cost"],
     "Subsidized transport passes are a strong idea, but universities should examine local travel patterns before promising a completely free service. A pass is valuable only if buses or trains actually reach the places students need at useful times. I would prioritize students with financial need and negotiate a discounted campus-wide option with the transport provider. The university could then compare usage and costs during a trial year. The first student's access argument is persuasive, especially for commuters who attend evening activities. Still, improving service frequency may sometimes be more useful than eliminating a small fare. Funding decisions should consider affordability, reliability, and coverage together so that the support changes what students can realistically access."),
    ('A city has set aside money for small neighborhood improvements. It is considering letting residents vote on proposals rather than leaving all selections to officials. The challenge is to combine local knowledge with fair attention to different kinds of need. Should cities use public voting to choose small improvement projects?', 'Voting gives residents a voice. Residents notice everyday problems that may not reach formal planning meetings. Voting could also make them more interested in following a project because they helped decide which proposal should receive support.', 'Popular ideas may overlook less visible needs. A popular project may attract votes because it is visible or enjoyable, not because it is most necessary. Small groups facing serious access problems could be outvoted by residents seeking a less urgent improvement.', ["voting", "projects", "residents"],
     "Public voting can help residents influence small improvement projects, provided that the choices have already been checked for feasibility and accessibility. Voting alone may favor a highly visible feature over essential work that benefits a smaller group. Before the vote, the city should explain each project's costs, users, and maintenance needs in comparable terms. It should also reserve funding for obligations that cannot depend on popularity, such as safe access. Residents could then choose among genuine options rather than attractive promises with hidden costs. For example, a neighborhood might compare a shaded seating area with a new play space using the same information format. This would make participation both meaningful and better informed."),
    ('Many students begin managing their own meals when they enter university. Some institutions offer practical workshops alongside academic courses, and a proposal to add basic cooking lessons has raised questions about the university’s role in everyday life skills. Should students learn basic cooking at university?', 'Cooking skills support independent living. Knowing a few dependable meals can make independent living less stressful. Students might otherwise rely on expensive prepared food simply because they do not know how to plan and prepare a simple alternative.', 'Universities should focus on academic subjects. A university has limited teaching space and staff. Skills that can be learned through family, community programs, or freely available resources should not displace the academic work students enrolled to study.', ["cooking", "students", "skills"],
     "Universities should offer optional basic cooking workshops rather than require a full course for every student. Learning a few affordable meals can support independent living, especially for students moving away from home for the first time. However, the second student is right that academic time is limited and that students arrive with different levels of experience. Short sessions organized with campus dining staff could cover practical tasks such as planning portions, storing food safely, and using inexpensive ingredients. Students would choose the sessions they need. This approach treats everyday skills as a useful support for university life while preserving flexibility and avoiding a requirement that may add little value for those who already cook confidently."),
    ('A workplace is considering allowing employees to choose their starting time within a limited range. Everyone would still work the same total hours, but colleagues would not always arrive and leave together. Managers need to assess the effects on the whole team. Should employers allow flexible starting times?', 'Flexibility can improve commuting and concentration. Avoiding the busiest commute could help people begin the day less stressed. Some employees also concentrate better earlier or later, so a fixed start time may not make the best use of their abilities.', 'Teams need overlapping working hours. Work often depends on a quick exchange with another person. If colleagues repeatedly miss each other, a small question may delay a whole task even though everyone is working the required hours.', ["flexible", "hours", "team"],
     "Employers should allow flexible starting times when the work can support them, with a shared period for collaboration. The two students' concerns are compatible: people can avoid the busiest commute while still being available for meetings and joint decisions. For example, a team might require everyone to be present from ten to three but let employees choose their remaining hours. Customer-facing roles would need a different arrangement to maintain coverage, so one rule should not be imposed on every job. Managers should evaluate completed work and service quality instead of treating identical arrival times as evidence of productivity. A trial with clear expectations would show whether flexibility helps the team without creating gaps in communication."),
    ('A school wants students to observe living systems rather than encounter science only in a textbook. Creating a garden could provide that opportunity, but the garden would remain a living space when classes end for weekends and holidays. Should school gardens be part of science education?', 'Gardens make biological processes visible. Students could observe changes over time and connect them with conditions they measure themselves. A plant that grows differently from a classmate’s can raise questions that a completed textbook diagram does not.', 'Maintenance can be difficult during holidays. A garden needs care even when no class is available to provide it. If maintenance depends on a few enthusiastic people, the project may decline when those people leave or become busy.', ["gardens", "science", "maintenance"],
     "School gardens can support science learning if they are designed around manageable investigations rather than simply as attractive spaces. Students could compare soil moisture, observe pollinators, or record how plants respond to different conditions. These activities connect classroom ideas with evidence collected over time. The holiday maintenance concern is important, so the garden should use suitable plants and have a realistic care plan before it is created. A small plot with clear responsibilities is better than a large neglected one. Schools could also partner with a community gardening group while keeping students responsible for their own measurements and explanations. The educational benefit depends on thoughtful use and sustained care, not on the presence of plants alone."),
    ('An instructor wants students to judge the strengths and weaknesses of their own work. One proposal is to make self-assessment a small part of the course grade, rather than using it only as an informal reflection activity. Should students be graded partly on self-assessment?', 'Self-assessment encourages reflection. Explaining why a piece of work meets a standard requires more thought than simply receiving a mark. Students may become better at identifying what to improve before submitting their next assignment.', 'Students may overestimate their performance. Students have different levels of confidence and may judge similar work very differently. A grade based partly on self-evaluation could reward overconfidence unless the instructor spends considerable time checking the judgments.', ["assessment", "reflection", "evidence"],
     "Self-assessment should contribute a modest part of a grade when students must support it with evidence. Simply asking students to choose their own mark would invite the concern raised by the second student. A more useful task is to identify one strength, one weakness, and specific work that demonstrates each. Instructors can assess the quality of that reflection rather than accepting the proposed score automatically. For example, a student might explain how a revised paragraph now connects evidence to a claim and acknowledge a remaining limitation. This process teaches students to judge their work using shared criteria. It complements external feedback and can make later independent study more effective without making grading depend mainly on confidence."),
    ('A city is reviewing the opening hours of its public libraries. Some residents want branches to remain open later, while others argue that the existing budget should improve daytime services. The choice involves both access and operating resources. Should cities offer more evening library hours?', 'Evening hours help workers and students. People working ordinary daytime hours may rarely get to use a branch before it closes. An evening visit could make borrowing, study space, and staff assistance available to residents who currently miss out.', 'Staffing and building costs would increase. Extending hours requires dependable staffing, not just leaving a door unlocked. If demand is low on some evenings, that money might help more people through better services during the busiest periods.', ["library", "hours", "costs"],
     "Cities should extend evening hours at selected libraries where demand is demonstrated. Workers and students may be unable to use a library during ordinary business hours, so access later in the day can make a meaningful difference. However, opening every branch late regardless of use would be expensive. A rotating schedule or a few transport-accessible branches could provide broader coverage at a lower cost. The city should track attendance, the services people use, and staff workload during a trial. It should also publish the schedule clearly so residents can plan. This targeted approach responds to the first student's access concern while taking the second student's operating-cost concern seriously rather than assuming resources are unlimited."),
    ('A university cafeteria is considering replacing some disposable takeaway containers with a returnable system. Students would be expected to bring containers back for washing, and the cafeteria would need to organize collection while maintaining convenient service. Should university cafeterias reduce the number of disposable containers?', 'Reusables can cut waste. A reusable container can serve many meals instead of becoming waste after one use. Seeing a return system every day could also make waste reduction feel like a normal shared responsibility.', 'Returning and washing containers may be inconvenient. Students often eat between classes or away from campus. Remembering another item and finding a return point could make an otherwise simple meal inconvenient, especially for people with irregular schedules.', ["containers", "reusable", "return"],
     "Cafeterias should introduce reusable containers through a convenient return system. Asking students to carry dirty containers all day would make the policy difficult to sustain, even if they support its environmental goal. Return points near residence halls and major classroom buildings could reduce that burden. A small refundable deposit would encourage returns, while clear washing procedures would address hygiene concerns. The program should begin with one dining location and measure loss rates, washing costs, and actual waste reduction. If students repeatedly struggle to return containers, the system should be adjusted before expansion. Successful reuse depends on the full routine of borrowing, carrying, returning, and cleaning, not simply on replacing one material with another."),
    ('Students often organize revision by completing many exercises on one topic before moving to the next. Another approach alternates related topics within a study period. We are considering what each arrangement helps a learner notice and practice. Should students study one topic intensively or alternate between related topics?', 'Intensive study allows deeper focus. Staying with one topic gives me time to understand its basic method before a different one competes for attention. Constant switching can feel confusing when I am still learning the first steps.', 'Alternating topics helps students recognize differences. A test does not always tell us which method a problem requires. Mixing related topics lets students practice noticing differences and choosing an approach, rather than repeating the method from the previous question.', ["study", "topics", "practice"],
     "Students should use both approaches at different stages. An unfamiliar topic may require a focused introduction so that its basic ideas make sense. Once students understand several related methods, alternating between them can help reveal when each is appropriate. For example, a statistics learner could first practice a new technique with guidance and later solve mixed problems that require choosing a method. Constant switching too early might create confusion, while studying one type of problem forever could produce a false sense of mastery. The key is to match practice to the learning goal. Teachers can gradually reduce support and mix tasks as understanding develops, combining focused preparation with the ability to make useful distinctions."),
    ('A community plans to commission public art for a shared space. Organizers are deciding how much residents should participate in the design, while the selected artist wants room to develop a coherent work rather than combine unrelated suggestions. Should public art projects involve local residents?', 'Participation builds a sense of ownership. Residents understand how the space is used and which local experiences matter to them. Being involved could make people more willing to care for the finished work instead of treating it as something imposed from outside.', 'Professional artists need creative freedom. An artist needs to make choices that fit together. If every resident expects a preferred image or idea to appear, the result may satisfy a checklist without becoming a strong work of art.', ["art", "residents", "artists"],
     "Local residents should have a role in public art projects without controlling every artistic decision. They understand how a space is used and can identify practical concerns such as access, visibility, or the meaning of local symbols. Artists, however, need room to turn those observations into a coherent work rather than a collection of unrelated requests. I would involve residents early through conversations about the place and its history, then ask the artist to explain how that input shaped the proposal. Clear boundaries would prevent consultation from becoming an expectation that everyone receives exactly what they suggested. This approach respects professional creativity while acknowledging that public art becomes part of a community's everyday environment."),
    ('Some students want to pause their university studies for a semester to work, volunteer, or explore another interest. Advisers are considering whether to actively encourage this option, recognizing that time away could affect both motivation and progress toward graduation. Should universities encourage students to take a gap semester?', 'Time away can clarify goals. Experiencing a different setting may help a student understand why a subject matters or decide on a direction. Returning with clearer goals could be more valuable than continuing through courses without a sense of purpose.', 'Interruptions can make returning difficult. Leaving a routine can make restarting harder, particularly when required courses follow a sequence. A student may intend to return promptly but discover that work, finances, or course availability complicates the plan.', ["semester", "goals", "return"],
     "Universities should make a gap semester possible with clear planning support, rather than encourage it as the best choice for everyone. Time away can help a student gain experience, address personal responsibilities, or reconsider academic goals. Yet the second student's concern matters: unclear credit rules or course sequences can make returning unnecessarily difficult. Before leaving, students should create a return plan with an adviser and understand any financial or scheduling consequences. A student pursuing a defined internship might benefit greatly, while someone leaving without a purpose may prefer a lighter course load. The university's role is to provide reliable options and continuity, allowing an informed decision that fits the individual's circumstances."),
    ('A local news organization has proposed working with a nearby university on reporting projects. Students could gain experience and researchers could explain complex topics, but both institutions have responsibilities that may not always point in the same direction. Should local news organizations partner with universities?', 'Students and researchers can contribute expertise. A reporter cannot be an expert in every subject. Access to researchers could improve explanations, while students working alongside journalists would learn to communicate clearly with people outside their academic fields.', 'Partnerships might compromise independence. A newsroom must sometimes criticize the university itself. A close partnership could make difficult reporting uncomfortable or lead audiences to wonder whether the organization is protecting an important source of support.', ["news", "universities", "independence"],
     "Partnerships between local news organizations and universities can be useful if editorial independence is protected explicitly. Researchers may help explain complex evidence, and students can gain experience gathering information responsibly. However, a university should not be allowed to approve coverage simply because it provides expertise or resources. The agreement should disclose funding, separate reporting decisions from institutional publicity, and allow journalists to investigate the university itself. For example, a data project about public transport could use academic methods while the newsroom decides which questions to pursue and how to present the findings. Transparency and clear roles would make the collaboration more trustworthy than either assuming expertise guarantees neutrality or rejecting cooperation altogether."),
    ('A parks department is considering allowing selected areas to grow more naturally instead of mowing and trimming them frequently. The proposal could change habitats and the appearance of public space, so the department must consider how visitors will understand it. Should city parks include areas with less intensive maintenance?', 'Natural areas can support wildlife. A variety of plants and less frequent disturbance could make room for species that do not thrive on a closely cut lawn. Parks can offer more than neat surfaces for human activities.', 'Visitors may see them as neglected. Without a clear explanation, visitors may think the city has stopped caring for the park. They may also avoid places that appear overgrown, reducing the usefulness of a space intended for everyone.', ["parks", "wildlife", "maintenance"],
     "Parks should include some less intensively maintained areas when their purpose is explained and access remains clear. Allowing selected grasses and plants to grow can create habitat that closely trimmed lawns do not provide. Nevertheless, visitors need to distinguish an intentional ecological area from genuine neglect. Maintained paths, visible boundaries, and concise signs can make that difference understandable. Managers should also monitor whether the area supports the intended species and whether unwanted plants are spreading. A varied park can contain both open lawns for recreation and quieter habitat zones. This approach responds to different uses rather than treating the entire space as either a formal garden or an untouched landscape with no management responsibilities."),
    ('A course currently bases much of its grade on one final examination. The instructor is considering several smaller assessments instead, without increasing the total amount of course content. Students differ in how they respond to repeated deadlines and feedback. Should courses replace one final exam with several smaller assessments?', 'Smaller assessments provide regular feedback. An early assessment shows students what they have misunderstood while there is still time to improve. One final test can reveal the same problem only after the course is effectively over.', 'Frequent deadlines may increase stress. Several courses making this change could produce a constant stream of deadlines. Students may spend all their time preparing the next assessed task rather than exploring ideas or recovering after demanding work.', ["assessments", "feedback", "deadlines"],
     "Several smaller assessments can improve learning, but their number and coordination matter. A single final exam may reveal misunderstandings too late for students to address them. Shorter tasks throughout the course can provide feedback while there is still time to improve. However, replacing one exam with constant high-stakes deadlines would create the stress described by the second student. I would combine a few meaningful assessments with low-stakes practice and publish the schedule early. Departments should also check for clusters of deadlines across required courses. The purpose is to create useful information about learning, not to measure students continuously. A balanced schedule can support revision and reduce dependence on performance during one particular day."),
    ('Students use digital devices for both coursework and social life. A university is considering setting aside more shared spaces where screens are not used, while keeping its usual study facilities available elsewhere. The goal is to support different ways of spending time. Should campuses provide more spaces without digital screens?', 'Screen-free spaces encourage concentration and conversation. When every nearby person is looking at a screen, it can be difficult to start a conversation or resist checking messages. A designated space would make an uninterrupted break easier to choose.', 'Students often need devices to work. A laptop may contain the reading or accessibility tool a student needs. Restricting devices can make a room less useful, and staff should be cautious about assuming that screen use always means distraction.', ["spaces", "screens", "students"],
     "Campuses should provide a small number of clearly designated spaces without digital screens while preserving plenty of ordinary study areas. Some students want a place to read printed material, think, or speak with friends without constant visual notifications. Others genuinely need laptops for assignments, so a campus-wide restriction would be impractical. A quiet lounge or a few tables could offer a voluntary choice rather than a judgment about how everyone should study. The policy should also accommodate assistive technology, since access needs differ. I would evaluate how the spaces are used before expanding them. Offering varied environments recognizes that concentration and social interaction depend partly on setting and that no single arrangement suits every task."),
    ('A university wants more students to volunteer with community organizations. It is considering formal recognition or other rewards, but organizers are discussing whether those incentives would improve participation or change the reasons students take part. Should universities reward students for volunteering?', 'Recognition encourages useful community work. Recognition shows that the university values work done beyond graded courses. It might persuade a student to try volunteering for the first time and discover an activity they then continue independently.', 'Rewards may weaken sincere motivation. People may focus on collecting a reward rather than responding to the organization’s actual needs. Short visits chosen for convenience could create work for coordinators without producing dependable long-term help.', ["volunteering", "recognition", "community"],
     "Universities should recognize sustained volunteering, but they should avoid turning service into a competition for the largest number of hours. A thoughtful reflection or a reference describing a student's contribution may be more meaningful than prizes based on totals. Community organizations need reliable help, not volunteers who choose only visible tasks that look impressive on an application. Recognition could therefore emphasize consistency, learning, and responsiveness to the organization's needs. For example, a student who maintains a weekly reading session may contribute more than someone who attends many unrelated events briefly. The concern about motivation is valid, but carefully designed recognition can acknowledge valuable work without making external rewards the central purpose of participation."),
    ('City budgets contain information that affects residents, but detailed financial documents can be difficult to read. Officials are considering short plain-language summaries to accompany the full documents, raising questions about accessibility and the risks of leaving information out. Should cities publish easy-to-read summaries of public budgets?', 'Summaries make decisions more understandable. Residents should not need specialist training to understand the main spending choices. A clear explanation could help people ask informed questions rather than rely only on headlines or isolated numbers from a debate.', 'Simplification can hide important details. A short summary must select what to emphasize, and that choice can shape a reader’s impression. Important obligations or trade-offs may disappear even when every sentence in the summary is technically accurate.', ["budgets", "summaries", "details"],
     "Cities should publish accessible budget summaries alongside the complete documents. Most residents cannot spend hours interpreting technical tables, yet they should be able to understand major spending choices and trade-offs. The second student's concern is a reason to design summaries carefully rather than avoid them. Each summary should state what is included, identify important uncertainties, and link directly to the relevant detailed figures. For example, a transport section could distinguish new construction from maintenance instead of combining them into one appealing number. Plain language can increase understanding without pretending that the budget is simple. Residents would then have a useful starting point and a clear path to deeper evidence when they want it."),
    ('A language program is organizing conversation partners. It could mainly pair learners at similar levels or deliberately mix beginners with more advanced speakers. Both arrangements could support practice, but they may create different opportunities and pressures. Should language learners practice with partners of similar ability?', 'Similar levels can make practice less intimidating. I am more willing to experiment when my partner is also learning. We can take time to find words without feeling that one person is slowing down someone who could speak much faster.', 'More advanced partners provide stronger examples. A stronger speaker can model expressions that neither beginner knows. If both learners repeatedly use the same incorrect form, they may reinforce it instead of noticing that another expression would be clearer.', ["language", "partners", "practice"],
     "Language learners benefit from a mixture of partners rather than a single rule about ability. Someone at a similar level may create a comfortable setting for taking risks and working through misunderstandings. A more advanced partner can offer useful language models, but the exchange becomes less valuable if that person does nearly all the speaking. The activity design matters as much as the level difference. Partners could take equal turns, prepare questions, and agree on when corrections are helpful. For example, a short conversation followed by two specific suggestions may be more productive than constant interruption. Teachers should help learners find exchanges that provide both participation and useful feedback, adjusting the balance as confidence develops."),
    ('A university is reviewing where its dining service buys food. Increasing purchases from nearby producers could affect local relationships, but the service must still provide affordable meals reliably across the academic year and meet students’ varied needs. Should universities buy locally produced food?', 'Local purchasing can support nearby communities. Regular university orders could give nearby producers a dependable customer. Students might also learn more about where their meals come from instead of seeing the dining service as disconnected from the surrounding community.', 'Price and year-round supply may be concerns. Local availability changes with the season, and a smaller supplier may not provide every item in the quantities required. A purchasing policy should not make meals substantially less affordable or dependable for students.', ["food", "local", "supply"],
     "Universities should include local producers in food purchasing when quality, cost, and reliable supply can be maintained. Supporting nearby businesses is a worthwhile goal, and seasonal purchases may create opportunities for students to learn about regional food systems. However, local origin alone does not settle every environmental or financial question. Dining services need enough food throughout the year and must keep meals affordable. I would start with flexible seasonal contracts for items that are produced reliably nearby, while retaining other suppliers for gaps. Publishing the reasons for purchasing choices would help students understand the trade-offs. A mixed strategy is more sustainable than a promise to buy everything locally regardless of practical conditions."),
    ('Office hours are intended to help students ask questions, yet some never attend. A department is considering requiring one visit each term to introduce students to this resource. Instructors must decide whether a compulsory visit would be useful for everyone. Should students be required to attend office hours once each term?', 'An initial visit can make seeking help easier. The first visit can feel intimidating if a student thinks office hours are only for people who are failing. A normal early conversation could make asking for help later much less difficult.', 'Compulsory visits may waste time for confident students. A student who understands the material may not have a meaningful question. Required visits could fill appointment slots with formalities while classmates with urgent problems wait longer for an instructor’s attention.', ["office", "hours", "help"],
     "I favor a brief introductory meeting for first-year students, but not a universal requirement every term. Many new students do not understand what office hours are for and may assume that asking questions signals failure. An initial conversation can show that discussing ideas and uncertainties is part of academic work. To avoid empty visits, students could bring one question about their learning goals or an upcoming assignment. After that introduction, attendance should normally be based on need, with online options for students whose schedules conflict. This approach addresses the access barrier identified by the first student while respecting the second student's concern that a repeated compulsory visit may provide little benefit for some learners."),
    ('A town is reviewing illuminated advertising after complaints about very bright screens at night. Businesses say visibility is important, while residents describe effects on nearby homes and streets. Officials must decide whether limits would be justified. Should towns limit bright advertising screens at night?', 'Limits can reduce distraction and light pollution. A screen can affect people who never choose to look at the advertisement, particularly those living beside it. Reducing brightness at night would respect their surroundings while leaving other ways to communicate a business’s location.', 'Businesses rely on visible advertising. A business open late needs customers to know it is there. Rules that are too restrictive could favor established shops that people already recognize over new businesses trying to attract their first customers.', ["screens", "night", "businesses"],
     "Towns should set reasonable nighttime brightness limits for advertising screens rather than ban them entirely. A sign can remain readable without illuminating nearby homes or distracting road users. The exact rules should consider location, operating hours, and surrounding light levels. For example, a screen beside residences might dim earlier than one in a busy commercial square, while both follow a maximum brightness standard. Businesses would benefit from clear, consistent requirements instead of unpredictable complaints. The town should consult affected groups and measure results after implementation. This would recognize the legitimate role of advertising while treating unnecessary nighttime light as a shared environmental issue that individual businesses cannot fully manage on their own."),
    ('Some instructors ask students to produce work for an audience outside the classroom, such as an exhibition or an information guide. The department is considering more of these projects, balancing their learning value against the responsibilities involved in public communication. Should university courses include public-facing projects?', 'Real audiences motivate careful communication. Knowing that somebody will actually use the work encourages students to explain terms and check assumptions. An instructor understands the course context already, but a real audience reveals gaps in an explanation much more clearly.', 'Public projects can expose inexperienced students to pressure. Students are still learning and need room to make mistakes. Public criticism or the possibility of misleading someone could make a project unnecessarily stressful, particularly before students have developed confidence in the subject.', ["projects", "audience", "students"],
     "Public-facing projects can be valuable when students receive support and the stakes are appropriate. Explaining a scientific idea to local visitors or preparing a clear community guide makes the need for accurate, accessible communication concrete. However, students should not be expected to publish sensitive or consequential claims without review. An instructor could begin with a small exhibition, provide examples, and check the final material before release. Students should also have a comparable alternative if public participation creates a serious barrier. The goal is learning through a meaningful audience, not publicity for its own sake. With a manageable scope and feedback, the project can strengthen understanding while limiting the pressure described by the second student."),
    ('An organization needs employees to learn new tools while continuing its ordinary work. Managers are considering offering short training courses during paid working hours instead of expecting staff to learn outside their scheduled time. The immediate and longer-term effects may differ. Should workplaces offer short courses during paid hours?', 'Training helps staff adapt to new tasks. Training is part of adapting to a job, so access should not depend on having free evenings. People with family responsibilities would otherwise find it harder to acquire the same skills as their colleagues.', 'It reduces time available for immediate work. A course can take several people away from urgent work at once. If the remaining staff must absorb every unfinished task, training could increase their workload before it produces any improvement in efficiency.', ["training", "work", "skills"],
     "Workplaces should reserve some paid time for training that is directly connected to employees' responsibilities. Expecting people to learn every new system after work can create unequal opportunities and lead to avoidable mistakes on the job. The concern about immediate productivity is real, so training should be focused and scheduled around service needs. A short course followed by supervised practice may be more useful than a full day of information that staff cannot apply. Managers should evaluate whether the training reduces errors or improves task quality over time. Learning is part of maintaining effective work, not simply time taken away from it, but courses should still have a clear purpose and observable benefits."),
    ('A writing instructor is deciding how much freedom students should have when selecting essay topics. A common assigned question makes work easier to compare, but individual topics could give students a stronger reason to investigate and develop an argument. Should students choose their own essay topics?', 'Choice increases interest and ownership. A topic connected with a genuine interest makes me more willing to read beyond the first few sources. That curiosity can support persistence when an argument becomes difficult to develop or revise.', 'Some students may select unmanageable topics. Interest does not guarantee a workable question. A student may choose a topic that is too broad or lacks accessible evidence, then spend most of the assignment trying to solve problems of scope.', ["topics", "essay", "scope"],
     "Students should have meaningful topic choice within a clear framework. Interest can sustain the effort needed to investigate and revise an essay, but complete freedom may lead a beginner toward a question that is too broad or lacks accessible evidence. A short proposal stage would solve much of this problem. Students could state their question, identify two possible sources, and explain what can reasonably be covered within the word limit. The instructor would then help refine the scope before substantial work begins. This preserves ownership while providing guidance. For example, a broad interest in migration could become a focused comparison of two explanations in the course readings, making independent choice compatible with the assignment's learning goals."),
    ('A city wants more residents to use buses, but some stops provide little protection from strong sunlight. Adding shade would use money and pavement space that could support other improvements. The city needs to consider where such an investment would be useful. Should cities invest in shaded bus stops?', 'Shade improves comfort while people wait. Waiting is part of a journey, not merely time before travel begins. A more comfortable stop could matter especially to people who cannot stand for long or choose a different departure time.', 'Shelters require maintenance and space. A shelter can obstruct a narrow pavement if it is poorly placed. Cleaning and repairs also continue after installation, so an attractive new structure is not a complete solution without a realistic maintenance arrangement.', ["bus", "shade", "maintenance"],
     "Cities should prioritize shaded bus stops where many people wait in exposed conditions. Comfortable waiting areas can make public transport more usable, especially during hot weather or for passengers who need to sit. However, adding structures without planning maintenance or pedestrian access would create new problems. The city should examine passenger numbers, sun exposure, available pavement width, and existing trees before selecting a design. Some locations may benefit from a roof, while others need seating or better placement of the stop. A maintenance budget should be part of the initial investment. This targeted approach treats shade as practical transport infrastructure rather than a decorative feature and responds to the second student's concerns about limited space and upkeep."),
    ('Universities traditionally organize many courses within individual departments. Some are now considering more courses that combine fields, asking students to connect methods and evidence from different disciplines. The issue is how breadth should relate to a student’s developing expertise. Should universities offer more interdisciplinary courses?', 'Complex problems require several perspectives. Problems such as managing a city cannot be understood through one subject alone. Students should practice listening to people whose methods differ from theirs rather than discover those differences only after graduation.', 'Students first need depth in a single subject. Combining subjects superficially can leave students using terminology they do not fully understand. It may be more useful to master a discipline’s reasoning before trying to judge how it relates to another field.', ["courses", "disciplines", "depth"],
     "Universities should offer interdisciplinary courses that build on, rather than replace, foundations in individual subjects. The second student is right that combining perspectives is difficult when students understand none of them well. However, advanced problems such as urban flooding involve scientific, economic, and social questions that one discipline alone may not answer. A well-designed course could bring students from different backgrounds together after they have completed introductory requirements. They would explain their methods, identify disagreements, and develop a joint analysis. Assessment should reward the quality of those connections instead of a superficial collection of vocabulary. Depth and breadth can reinforce one another when the course makes clear what each perspective contributes and where its limits remain."),
    ('A neighborhood group wants to lend residents equipment that most households use only occasionally. Sharing could reduce duplicate purchases, but the arrangement would depend on people trusting that the equipment will be available and returned in usable condition. Should communities share equipment for occasional use?', 'Sharing reduces expense and storage needs. A household may need a tool for one afternoon and then store it for years. Sharing would free space and make useful equipment available to residents who cannot justify buying it individually.', 'Responsibility for damage can be unclear. Damage is not always obvious when an item is returned. If people disagree about who caused a problem or who should pay, one incident could make neighbors reluctant to lend or borrow again.', ["equipment", "sharing", "responsibility"],
     "Communities can benefit from sharing equipment if responsibility is defined before lending begins. Many items take up space while being used only occasionally, so a small borrowing system could save money and reduce duplicate purchases. The second student's concern is important because one damaged item can create conflict and discourage participation. A simple condition check at collection and return, clear instructions, and a modest maintenance fund would help. The system should begin with durable, low-risk items rather than expensive or complex machinery. Members could review the rules after a trial period. Sharing works best when expectations are visible and practical, allowing people to trust the process rather than relying only on informal goodwill."),
    ('An instructor is considering assignments in which students create study guides or short explanations for other learners. Giving academic credit could encourage careful work, but the resulting resources may be used by people who assume the information is dependable. Should students receive credit for creating educational resources?', 'Teaching others strengthens understanding. Explaining an idea for someone else forces the writer to decide what matters and where a reader might become confused. That can reveal weaknesses in the writer’s own understanding more effectively than copying notes.', 'Student-made materials may contain errors. A clear layout and confident tone can make an incorrect explanation look trustworthy. If other students study from it, a small misunderstanding may spread beyond the person who originally made the mistake.', ["resources", "students", "review"],
     "Students should receive credit for creating educational resources when the work includes a careful review process. Explaining a concept for others forces the author to organize ideas and notice gaps in understanding. However, an attractive guide can still contain mistakes, so publication should follow feedback from peers and an instructor. The assessment could reward source quality, clarity, revision, and responsiveness to comments rather than visual polish alone. For example, students might produce a short guide to interpreting graphs, test it with classmates, and correct points that caused confusion. This turns the risk of error into part of the learning process while ensuring that the final resource is appropriate for the audience who will use it."),
    ('A city has older buildings that no longer serve their original purposes. Some residents want them adapted for new uses, while others question whether renovation is practical. Decisions must consider both the character of the place and the needs of future users. Should cities preserve old buildings by giving them new uses?', 'Reuse maintains local character and avoids demolition. Keeping a building in use preserves a visible connection with local history. Residents may value familiar spaces even when a new activity occupies them, and reuse can avoid discarding the entire existing structure.', 'Adaptation may be costly and technically difficult. An old building may require major work before it is safe and accessible for a new purpose. If adaptation consumes the whole budget, the final service could be less useful than a purpose-built alternative.', ["buildings", "reuse", "cost"],
     "Reusing old buildings is often worthwhile, but preservation decisions should consider both public value and the practical demands of a new use. A former warehouse might become a library or community workspace while retaining features that connect residents with local history. Yet not every building can be adapted safely or affordably for every purpose. Structural condition, accessibility, energy performance, and long-term maintenance should be assessed openly. I would compare realistic reuse options with replacement rather than assuming that age alone settles the decision. A successful project preserves meaningful character while serving current needs. Clear evidence about costs and benefits can help communities make a durable choice instead of choosing between nostalgia and demolition as if no other possibilities existed."),
]
